<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.3.2: Writing Equations for Patterns with Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01777</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.3.2: Writing Equations for Patterns with Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2d090c20-e1d6-493f-be9a-581ed4885c13</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
          <media alt="Three steps of a growing pattern.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_03_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">If the pattern continues, what will we see in Step 5 and Step 18?</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Sketch or describe the figure in each of these steps.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Step 5 is a collection of 25 small squares, in a 5-by-5 arrangement, surrounded by 4 other small squares at the corners. Step 18 is a collection of \(18^2\) small squares, in an 18 by 18 arrangement, surrounded by 4 other small squares at the corners.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">How many small squares are in each of these steps? Explain how you know.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Step 5: 29, because it is \(5^2+4\) or \((5 \cdot 5)+4\). Step 18: 328, because it is \(18^2+4\) or \((18 \cdot 18)+4\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Write an equation to represent the relationship between the step number \(n\) and the number of squares \(y\). Be prepared to explain how each part of your equation relates to the pattern. (If you get stuck, try making a table.)</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">\(y=n^2+4\) or equivalent. The \(n^2\) term represents the number of small squares in the central \(n\)-by-\(n\) large square. The 4 represents the 4 small squares that are added to the large square at its 4 corners.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Sketch the first three steps of a pattern that can be represented by the equation \(y=n^2-1\).</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">An \(n\)-by-\(n\) array of squares with one square missing at the top right. The first step (for \(n=1\)) will have no squares.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">
                  <media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES SHOWING STEPS 1 THROUGH 3. STEP 1 DOES NOT SHOW ANYTHING. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 1 ROW AND 2 COLUMNS. 1 SQUARE IS ADDED TO THE TOP LEFT. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 3 COLUMNS. 2 SQUARES ARE ADDED TO THE TOP LEFT.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_03_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <para id="para-00020">For the original step pattern in the statement, \(y=n^2+4\), write an equation to represent the relationship between the step number, \(n\), and the perimeter, \(P\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00022">\(P=4n+16\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00023">For the step pattern you created in Part 3 of the activity, \(y=n^2-1\), write an equation to represent the relationship between the step number, \(n\), and the perimeter, \(P\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00025">If the pattern was an \(n\)-by-\(n\) array of squares with a square missing from the corner, the perimeter could be given by \(P=0\) when \(n=1\), and by \(P=4n\) when \(n>1\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00026">Are these linear functions?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Yes, in both cases, these are linear functions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00029">
          <media alt="Three steps of a growing pattern. Step 1: Total of two squares, stacked one atop the other. Step 2: Total of five squares, two squares on the bottom row and the second row and one square on the top row. Step 3: Total of ten squares, three squares on the bottom row, row 2, and row 3, and one square on the top row.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_04_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00030">Which equation represents the relationship between the step number \(n\) and the number of squares \(y\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title> Patterns Represented by Quadratics</title>
        <para id="para-00031">See Steps 1 – 3 of a pattern of squares below. Write an equation representing the relationship between the step number \(n\) and the number of squares \(y\).</para>
        <para id="para-00032">
              <media alt="">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_05_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>
        <para id="para-00033">Make a table for the number of columns and rows of each step and the number of squares:</para>
        <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="4">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> Step </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> # of columns </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> # of rows </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> # of squares </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 1 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 2 </entry>
                <entry> 6 </entry>
                <entry> 12 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
                <entry> 3 </entry>
                <entry> 9 </entry>
                <entry> 27 </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry> \(n\) </entry>
                <entry> \(n\) </entry>
                <entry> \(3n\) </entry>
                <entry> \(3n^2\) </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00034">First, count how many columns there are in each step (the length):</para>
        <para id="para-00035">For each, there are \(n\) columns.</para>
        <para id="para-00036">Next, count how many rows there are in each step (the height):</para>
        <para id="para-00037">For each, there are \(3n\) rows.</para>
        <para id="para-00038">The area of this figure is then \(l \times h\) or \(n \times 3n\), which becomes \(y=3n^2\).</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Patterns Represented by Quadratics</title>
      <para id="para-00039">See Steps 1 – 3 of a pattern of squares below. Write an equation representing the relationship between the step number \(n\) and the number of squares \(y\).</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
            <problem id="prob-00008">
              <para id="para-00040">
                          <media alt="">
                    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_05_02.jpg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
              <para id="para-00041">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00008">
              <para id="para-00042">Here is how to represent this pattern as a quadratic:</para>
              <para id="para-00043">Make a table for the number of columns and rows of each step and the number of squares:</para>
              <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00002">
                <tgroup cols="4">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> Step </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> # of columns </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> # of rows </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> # of squares </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 1 </entry>
                      <entry> 0 </entry>
                      <entry> 0 </entry>
                      <entry> 0 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 2 </entry>
                      <entry> 2 </entry>
                      <entry> 2 </entry>
                      <entry> 3 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 3 </entry>
                      <entry> 3 </entry>
                      <entry> 3 </entry>
                      <entry> 8 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> \(n\) </entry>
                      <entry> \(n\) </entry>
                      <entry> \(n\) </entry>
                      <entry> \(n^2-1\) </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
              <para id="para-00044">For this pattern, notice that the area would be the number of columns times the number of rows, but then one block is subtracted.</para>
              <para id="para-00045">Note that for Step 1, there was a \(1 \times 1\) block there, but then it was subtracted with the equation given.</para>
              <para id="para-00046">The equation, then, is \(y=n^2-1\).</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
